# Minimal Design Strategy Guide

## 1. Folder and File Structure
- **CSS:** Place all minimal theme CSS in `static/css/minimal-css/`, organized by feature (e.g., `theme/`, `nav/`, `calendar/`, `tooltip/`, `calculator/`).
- **JS:** Place all minimal theme JS in `static/js/minimal-js/`, organized by feature (e.g., `nav/`, `tooltip/`, `calculator/`).
- **Components:** Place reusable HTML components in `templates/minimal/components/` (e.g., `nav.html`, `calculator.html`).
- **Base Template:** All minimal pages should extend `minimal/base.html`.

## 2. Base Template Usage
- Use `minimal/base.html` for all minimal UI pages.
- Include nav and calculator as `{% include %}` components.
- Load Bootstrap CSS, Quicksand (and any logo font), and all JavaScript libraries in the base.
- Use named `{% endblock %}`s and double quotes in template tags for lint compliance.

## 3. Font and Color Guidelines
- **Primary font:** Quicksand for all UI text.
- **Logo font:** Orbitron (or similar) for the app name (monAI), styled as a logo.
- **Color palette:** Use soft backgrounds, glassmorphism, and accent colors (#2196f3 blue, #4caf50 green, #ffd600 yellow, #e0e0e0 gray).
- **Gradients:** Use subtle gradients for backgrounds and logo text.

## 4. JavaScript Library Strategy

### 4.1 Library Loading Order
Load libraries in this specific order in `minimal/base.html`:
```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/alpinejs@3.13.10/dist/cdn.min.js" defer></script>
<script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js"></script>
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.1.3/dist/js/bootstrap.min.js"></script>
<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/animejs/3.2.1/anime.min.js"></script>
```

### 4.2 Alpine.js - Declarative State Management
**Use for:**
- Simple UI state management (toggles, show/hide)
- Reactive data binding in templates
- Built-in transitions for simple animations
- Form validation and local state

**Examples:**
```html
<!-- Toggle with state management -->
<div x-data="{ open: false }">
    <button @click="open = !open">
        <span x-text="open ? 'Hide' : 'Show'"></span>
    </button>
    <div x-show="open" x-transition.duration.300ms>Content</div>
</div>

<!-- Form validation -->
<div x-data="{
    email: '',
    errors: {},
    validate() { /* validation logic */ }
}">
    <input x-model="email" @blur="validate()">
    <span x-show="errors.email" x-text="errors.email"></span>
</div>
```

**Best Practices:**
- Keep Alpine.js logic simple and declarative
- Use for local component state, not global application state
- Leverage built-in transitions for simple show/hide animations
- Avoid complex business logic in Alpine.js

### 4.3 Anime.js - Complex Animations
**Use for:**
- Page load animations and entrance effects
- Complex multi-property animations
- Staggered animations for lists and grids
- Interactive element animations (hover, click feedback)
- Modal and overlay animations

**Examples:**
```javascript
// Page load animation
anime({
    targets: '.header',
    opacity: [0, 1],
    translateY: [-30, 0],
    duration: 800,
    easing: 'easeOutCubic'
});

// Staggered list animation
anime({
    targets: '.list-item',
    opacity: [0, 1],
    translateY: [20, 0],
    delay: anime.stagger(100, {start: 200}),
    duration: 600,
    easing: 'easeOutCubic'
});

// Interactive feedback
anime({
    targets: button,
    scale: [1, 1.08],
    duration: 180,
    easing: 'easeOutCubic'
});
```

**Best Practices:**
- Use hardware-accelerated properties (opacity, transform, scale)
- Keep animations under 800ms for page loads, under 200ms for interactions
- Use appropriate easing functions: `easeOutCubic` for entrances, `easeInOutQuad` for toggles
- Avoid animating layout-triggering properties (width, height, margin, padding)

### 4.4 jQuery - DOM Manipulation and AJAX
**Use for:**
- AJAX requests and server communication
- Complex DOM manipulation and traversal
- Event handling for dynamic content
- Integration with third-party libraries (Bootstrap, Select2)
- Legacy code compatibility

**Examples:**
```javascript
// AJAX requests
$.ajax({
    url: '/api/data',
    type: 'POST',
    data: formData,
    success: function(response) {
        // Handle response
    }
});

// DOM manipulation
$('.element').fadeIn(300).slideDown(300);

// Event delegation
$(document).on('click', '.dynamic-button', function() {
    // Handle dynamic content
});
```

**Best Practices:**
- Use for server communication and complex DOM operations
- Prefer Alpine.js for simple state management
- Prefer Anime.js for complex animations
- Use event delegation for dynamic content
- Keep jQuery usage focused on its strengths

### 4.5 Library Coherence and Integration
**Working Together:**
```html
<!-- Alpine.js handles state, Anime.js handles animation -->
<div x-data="{ visible: false }">
    <button @click="visible = !visible; animateContent()">Toggle</button>
    <div x-show="visible" x-init="if(visible) animateIn()">
        Content
    </div>
</div>

<script>
function animateIn() {
    anime({
        targets: '.content',
        opacity: [0, 1],
        translateY: [20, 0],
        duration: 600,
        easing: 'easeOutCubic'
    });
}
</script>
```

**Conflict Avoidance:**
- Alpine.js loads first (deferred) to initialize early
- jQuery and Anime.js load after for DOM manipulation and animations
- Each library handles its specific domain
- No direct conflicts between libraries

## 5. Animation and Interactivity Guidelines
- **Page Load:** Use Anime.js for entrance animations (opacity, translateY, scale)
- **Interactive Feedback:** Use Anime.js for hover/click effects (scale, color changes)
- **State Changes:** Use Alpine.js transitions for simple show/hide
- **Complex Sequences:** Use Anime.js with staggered delays and multiple properties
- **Performance:** Prefer CSS transforms over layout properties
- **Accessibility:** Ensure animations respect `prefers-reduced-motion`

## 6. Nav and Component Modularity
- Nav and calculator must be in their own HTML, CSS, and JS files.
- Nav should be responsive, with a hamburger menu on mobile.
- App name (monAI) is not a link, styled as a logo.
- Use Bootstrap for layout, override with minimal CSS for theme.

## 7. Accessibility and Responsiveness
- Ensure all interactive elements are keyboard accessible.
- Use `aria-current="page"` for active nav links.
- Maintain high color contrast and readable font sizes.
- All layouts must be responsive (test on mobile and desktop).
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` for animations.

## 8. Linting and Code Style
- Use named `{% endblock %}`s and double quotes in all template tags.
- Follow project linting rules for CSS and JS (indentation, quotes, etc.).
- Use semantic HTML and ARIA attributes where appropriate.
- Keep JavaScript modular and feature-specific.

## 9. Extending the Theme
New pages/components should:
- Extend `minimal/base.html`.
- Place feature CSS/JS in the appropriate minimal-css/minimal-js subfolder.
- Use modular, reusable components where possible.
- Follow established font, color, and animation standards.
- Choose appropriate JavaScript library for each interaction type.

## 10. General Principles
- Prioritize clarity, whitespace, and minimalism.
- Use glassmorphism and soft shadows for depth.
- Avoid unnecessary complexity—keep UI and code simple and maintainable.
- Use the right tool for the job: Alpine.js for state, Anime.js for animations, jQuery for AJAX.
- Document new components and patterns in this strategy file as the theme evolves.

## Page Height and Background Standards

### ✅ **Height Fix Strategy (Implemented)**
Currently, pages need individual height fixes until we perfect the global solution:
- **Base Template**: Has `page-content-wrapper` class but needs refinement
- **Theme CSS**: Has global styles but may need page-specific overrides
- **Current Approach**: Use page-specific containers with `min-height: 100vh` and background gradient

### 🎯 **For New Pages**

#### **Template Structure (Required)**
```html
{% extends "minimal/base.html" %}
{% load static %}

{% block extra_css %}
<!-- Page-specific CSS here -->
{% endblock extra_css %}

{% block content %}
<div class="[page-name]-container">
  <!-- Your page content here -->
  <!-- Add page-specific container for height fix -->
</div>
{% endblock content %}

{% block extra_js %}
<!-- Page-specific JS here -->
{% endblock extra_js %}
```

#### **CSS Guidelines**
- **DO** add height and background fixes to page-specific CSS for now
- **DO** use the existing theme classes and patterns
- **DO** follow the established folder structure:
  ```
  static/css/minimal-css/[feature]/[page].css
  static/js/minimal-js/[feature]/[page].js
  ```
- **DO** add page-specific container styles:
  ```css
  .[page-name]-container {
    min-height: 100vh;
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #f3f6fa 0%, #e9eafc 100%) !important;
  }
  ```

### 🚫 **Common Mistakes to Avoid**

1. **Don't forget to add page-specific height containers** - Currently needed until global fix is perfected
2. **Don't override background gradients** - Use theme colors
3. **Don't use different background gradients** - Stick to the standard gradient

### ✅ **What's Already Handled**

- ✅ Full viewport height coverage
- ✅ Consistent background gradient
- ✅ Navigation height compensation
- ✅ Bottom padding for content
- ✅ Responsive behavior

### 🔧 **If You Need Custom Heights**

For special cases (like modals or overlays), use:
```css
.custom-overlay {
  min-height: 100vh;
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, #f3f6fa 0%, #e9eafc 100%);
}
```

